00:00:08.759 --> 00:00:11.839
Today's topic is: How similar are French and Italian?

00:00:11.899 --> 00:00:13.479
Well, they are actually very similar.

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:16.859
They share a lot of common vocabulary 
and the grammar is very similar.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:19.539
But, in terms of phonology, they are quite different.

00:00:19.679 --> 00:00:23.099
So, in their spoken form, 
they are generally not mutually intelligible.

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:25.639
French and Italian are both Romance Languages.

00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:29.539
They both developed out of the Vulgar Latin 
spoken throughout the Roman Empire.

00:00:29.659 --> 00:00:33.299
But, French kind of stands out as different 
from other Romance Languages.

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:36.779
The historical land of Gaul 
is located inside modern day France.

00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:39.819
And one theory is that, 
when the Roman Empire  conquered Gaul

00:00:39.819 --> 00:00:45.299
and tried to force the local population to speak Latin,
they spoke it using Gaulish speech sounds

00:00:45.439 --> 00:00:49.159
And that has affected the phonology and the pronunciation of French until this day.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:50.819
Gaulish was a Celtic language.

00:00:50.819 --> 00:00:54.079
So that means that there is 
some Celtic influence in the French language.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.599
Specifically, in phonology.

00:00:55.759 --> 00:00:58.719
In any case, French and Italian are very closely related.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:00.919
Let's have a look at just how close they are.

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:01.999
Vocabulary.

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:05.319
Italian and French are very similar 
in terms of vocabulary.

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:08.939
The lexical similarity of the two languages
is eighty-nine percent.

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:13.619
That means that 89 % of the words used
have equivalents or cognates in the other language.

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:17.179
That doesn't mean the words are exactly
 the same but they are clearly related.

00:01:17.319 --> 00:01:18.579
Some obvious examples:

00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:20.679
Good day / Hello

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:23.019
Interesting

00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:25.379
To eat.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:27.879
And there are some less obvious cognates too.

00:01:28.459 --> 00:01:30.219
Hospital

00:01:30.679 --> 00:01:32.119
hour

00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:34.399
At what time?

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:36.839
To search (for), look for.

00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:42.899
I think the figure of 89% is probably true 
if we include formal literary vocabulary.

00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:45.559
But in daily conversation, it seems to be a little lower.

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:46.899
Let's look at these examples:

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:56.059
[...]

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:00.759
So here you can see that most of the words have 
an equivalent except for the words that are in red.

00:02:00.759 --> 00:02:01.959
Those ones are different.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:05.799
Like the word for "here". 
In Italian, it's "qui" and in French, it's "ici".

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:09.959
And the word for "with". 
In Italian, it's "con" and in French, it's "avec".

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:13.659
The word for "in".
In Italian, it's "in" and in French, it's "dans"

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:17.059
Aside from those words, the other words
 are all basically equivalent.

00:02:17.219 --> 00:02:20.339
Except for a couple of definite articles
which are used differently

00:02:20.479 --> 00:02:25.079
I recommend that you pause the video now. Look at 
the two sentences along with an English break down.

00:02:25.259 --> 00:02:27.499
And see what you notice about the equivalence.

00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:30.779
Here are another couple examples.

00:02:31.079 --> 00:02:37.259
 

00:02:37.899 --> 00:02:41.279
So again, these sentences are quite similar 
but a few of the words are different.

00:02:41.499 --> 00:02:46.299
"dimenticate": that means "forget" in Italian
and "oubliez" means "forget" in French.

00:02:46.419 --> 00:02:51.159
And again, we see the different words for "in".
The Italian word "in" and the French word "dans".

00:02:51.419 --> 00:02:56.879
And we see different words for "store".
The Italian word "negozio" and the French word "magasin"

