So again, why don't you pause the video
and have a look at the two sentences,

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:02.959
and see what you can learn by comparing them?

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:09.279
So you can see that, in a typical sentence, 
the majority  of vocabulary used is similar

00:03:09.279 --> 00:03:12.539
to the vocabulary used in the other language.
But not all of it.

00:03:12.919 --> 00:03:13.539
Grammar

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:17.799
In French and Italian, the word order and 
the sentence structure are generally very similar.

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:20.279
Here's a simple sentence to show similar word order.

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:22.859
The sentence means "I have a newspaper for the trip"

00:03:27.759 --> 00:03:31.099
And you can see that these sentences 
are - word for word - translatable.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.559
But there are also some grammatical differences too.

00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:38.719
In French, subject pronouns are consistently used.

00:03:39.039 --> 00:03:42.919
But, in Italian, subject pronouns generally aren't used, 
except for emphasis.

00:03:43.059 --> 00:03:47.179
So, in French: "..." , In Italian "..."
I speak English.

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:50.999
But "Io" is not usually said. So it's just "Parlo inglese"

00:03:52.079 --> 00:03:53.979
And there's another difference in pronouns.

00:03:54.039 --> 00:03:57.739
In French, the 2nd person formal 
and plural pronoun is "vous".

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:02.479
In Italian, the third person pronoun "Lei" 
is used for the second person formal.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:05.839
So, in French we'd have:

00:04:06.539 --> 00:04:09.339
but in Italian:

00:04:09.539 --> 00:04:12.559
So, the Italian sentence is like "SHE speaks too slowly"

00:04:12.699 --> 00:04:16.239
But it should be clear from the context
that you are saying "you" and not "she",

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:17.939
because you're talking to someone directly.

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:25.099
Also, in Italian, direct object pronouns are also normally 
attached to the end of a verb when it's in the infinitive form.

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:27.399
In French,  we'd have :

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:31.119
but, in Italian, we'd have:

00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:34.979
Negation is also a little different in the two languages.

00:04:35.159 --> 00:04:39.299
In French, we'd say:
with "ne" and "pas" to show negation.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.499
But, in Italian, we'd say:

00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:46.739
So, in French, we have "ne" and "pas" 
but, in Italian, there's no equivalent of "pas"

00:04:47.939 --> 00:04:52.319
Those are some little differences, but other major things 
like the verb tenses are basically the same.

00:04:52.499 --> 00:04:55.279
French and Italian share the same 14  verb tenses.

00:04:55.399 --> 00:04:59.559
Of course, they're not conjugated exactly the same 
but the tenses function in the same way.

00:04:59.559 --> 00:05:02.139
For example, let's look at how the past tense is formed.

00:05:02.459 --> 00:05:07.279
In French "..." , in Italian "..."
(You have eaten something)

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:10.919
This means "You ate something",
spoken to one person informally

00:05:11.139 --> 00:05:12.419
The verb consists of two parts:

00:05:12.479 --> 00:05:17.359
First, the 2nd person singular form of the word for "have" 
and second, the past participle

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:22.579
Obviously, the forms are different but the way you use 
them together to create the past tense is the same.

00:05:22.899 --> 00:05:26.319
And just as a quick side note, 
notice how to say "something" in the two languages:

00:05:26.619 --> 00:05:29.239
"quelque chose" versus "qualcosa".

00:05:29.579 --> 00:05:33.799
French and Italian grammar is very similar,
just like the vocabulary is largely similar

00:05:33.859 --> 00:05:34.659
Pronunciation

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:40.819
The biggest difference between French and Italian 
and the one that prevents mutual intelligibility the most 
is pronunciation

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:44.919
Italian pronunciation is mostly phonetic 
and mirrors the written words exactly

00:05:45.179 --> 00:05:47.619
French words are pronounced differently
 from the way they're written.

00:05:47.719 --> 00:05:50.639
And you have to keep some phonetic rules 
in mind when pronouncing words.

00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:55.299
Most Italian words end in a vowel, which makes it easy 
to pronounce the end of the word.

00:05:55.559 --> 00:05:59.219
In French, there are many consonants,
that are usually silent at the end of a word

00:05:59.439 --> 00:06:01.959
For example, this sentence meaning "the weather is called"

00:06:02.259 --> 00:06:05.239
In Italian "...", in French "..."

00:06:05.599 --> 00:06:08.459
Notice that the "d" sound at the end of "froid" is silent.

00:06:09.399 --> 00:06:15.199
Another example is the phrase for "white wine" 
In Italian "vino bianco" ; in French "vin blanc"

00:06:15.519 --> 00:06:20.559
Hear that the final "c" is silent. So the last sound we hear 
is the nasal sound represented by "n".

00:06:20.799 --> 00:06:22.559
"Vin" also ends in a nasal sound.

00:06:22.739 --> 00:06:27.119
The "n" is not pronounced as a consonant here.
Instead, it nasalizes the preceding vowel

00:06:27.279 --> 00:06:29.959
So rather than "..." , it's "..."

00:06:30.959 --> 00:06:33.539
There are also some other sounds 
that are quite different in French.

00:06:33.539 --> 00:06:37.719
For example the French "r". It's not the same 
as the rolled "r" that you would find in Italian.

00:06:37.899 --> 00:06:43.219
In Italian, you produce the r by flapping the tip of 
your tongue against the roof of your mouth behind your teeth

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:46.619
But, the French "r" is a guttural "r" 
that's produced at the back of your mouth.

00:06:47.839 --> 00:06:50.919
In Italian "...", in French ""  (to go away, to leave)

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:58.059
So those final silent consonants, as well as the nasal sounds, 
the French "r", those are some differences with Italian.

00:06:58.059 --> 00:07:02.019
but they're are also some of the things that learners 
find challenging about French pronunciation.

00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:07.079
Let's finish by just breaking down a couple more sentences 
and seeing what similarities and differences we notice.

00:07:07.219 --> 00:07:09.499
Here's a sentence meaning:
"What are your favorite dishes?"

00:07:09.699 --> 00:07:15.259
In Italian "..." ; in French "..."

00:07:15.439 --> 00:07:20.919
So, word by word, the sentence is: 
what are your (plural) dishes (plural) preferred (plural)

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:25.839
"Quali" and "Quels" are equivalents,
but notice that the plural ending is different.

00:07:25.959 --> 00:07:30.559
In Italian, it's often "i" or "e"
but, in French, it's a silent "s"

00:07:30.759 --> 00:07:32.439
"sono" and "sont" are equivalent

00:07:32.659 --> 00:07:37.419
but notice that "sono" ends in a vowel 
while "sont" ends in a nasal sound a silent "t".

00:07:37.679 --> 00:07:39.559
"i vostri" and "vos" are equivalent

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:44.079
but, in Italian, the definite article is normally 
used with a possessive pronoun like "vostri",

00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:46.199
while, in French, there's no definite article.

00:07:46.439 --> 00:07:48.199
"piatti" and "plat" are equivalent

00:07:48.459 --> 00:07:54.239
but notice the different plural endings and notice 
that words with "i" an Italian often have an "l" in French.

00:07:54.559 --> 00:07:58.559
We've seen another example of that:
 "bianco" in Italian and "blanc" in French.

00:07:59.859 --> 00:08:05.419
"preferiti" and "préférés" are obvious equivalents, 
just with different accent markings and a different ending.

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:10.559
Now, here's one more pair of sentences 
but, this time, I want you to have a look at them

00:08:10.619 --> 00:08:14.039
and notice the similarities and differences 
without me breaking it down for you.

00:08:14.039 --> 00:08:14.859
in Italian

00:08:17.759 --> 00:08:18.499
in French  !!! déjeuner (with é accent) !!!

00:08:22.379 --> 00:08:25.959
So I hope this video has made it clear just 
how similar French and Italian really are.

00:08:26.019 --> 00:08:29.299
The speaker of one of them can probably
learn the other quite easily.

00:08:29.399 --> 00:08:32.759
And, in writing, the level of mutual intelligibility is quite high.

00:08:32.759 --> 00:08:38.539
But, because the cognate words are all a little bit different, 
because the pronunciation of the two languages is so different,

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:41.959
the two languages are not mutually 
intelligible in their spoken form.

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:47.359
But, as a learner of Italian, my previous knowledge of French
makes a lot of things in Italian instantly clear to me.

00:08:47.499 --> 00:08:50.619
So, the question of the day 
for speakers of French and Italian:

00:08:50.619 --> 00:08:53.339
how similar do you consider the two languages to be?

00:08:53.479 --> 00:08:55.159
And to learners of French and Italian:

